{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.e9417aaf8d","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-9$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$6$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 2) - 6$. What is the value of $g(2)$?</p>","answer":["0"],"explanation":"$g(2) = f(-(2) + 2) - 6 = f(0) - 6$. From the table, $f(0) = 6$, so $g(2) = 6 - 6 = 0$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.bde85f55be","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$0$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 2) + 4$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?</p>","answer":["4"],"explanation":"$g(-2) = f(-((-2)) + 2) + 4 = f(4) + 4$. From the table, $f(4) = 0$, so $g(-2) = 0 + 4 = 4$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.7aa91a419b","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) + 5$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?</p>","answer":["3"],"explanation":"$g(-2) = f(2((-2)) + 2) + 5 = f(-2) + 5$. From the table, $f(-2) = -2$, so $g(-2) = -2 + 5 = 3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.803817c151","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$10$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) - 3$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["-9"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) + 1) - 3 = f(3) - 3$. From the table, $f(3) = -6$, so $g(1) = -6 - 3 = -9$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.74db43afe2","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x - 1) - 1$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["-7"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(-(1) - 1) - 1 = f(-2) - 1$. From the table, $f(-2) = -6$, so $g(1) = -6 - 1 = -7$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.cfbd988fd7","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$5$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 2) + 2$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["7"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) + 2) + 2 = f(4) + 2$. From the table, $f(4) = 5$, so $g(1) = 5 + 2 = 7$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b380226bdb","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$6$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) + 5$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["3"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) - 1) + 5 = f(-1) + 5$. From the table, $f(-1) = -2$, so $g(0) = -2 + 5 = 3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b2c24db335","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$3$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-6$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-4$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-3$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 1) - 5$. What is the value of $g(-2)$?</p>","answer":["6"],"explanation":"$g(-2) = f(-((-2)) + 1) - 5 = f(3) - 5$. From the table, $f(3) = 11$, so $g(-2) = 11 - 5 = 6$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.2e847353b5","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$9$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-5$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$4$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 1) + 4$. What is the value of $g(2)$?</p>","answer":["13"],"explanation":"$g(2) = f(-(2) + 1) + 4 = f(-1) + 4$. From the table, $f(-1) = 9$, so $g(2) = 9 + 4 = 13$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.e181353b56","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$11$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$-1$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$10$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$8$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$1$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) - 3$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["-1"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) + 1) - 3 = f(3) - 3$. From the table, $f(3) = 2$, so $g(1) = 2 - 3 = -1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.17bee67d6a","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$4$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$7$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x + 1) + 3$. What is the value of $g(1)$?</p>","answer":["10"],"explanation":"$g(1) = f(2(1) + 1) + 3 = f(3) + 3$. From the table, $f(3) = 7$, so $g(1) = 7 + 3 = 10$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.27d9743176","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$9$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$12$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$6$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$12$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$1$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$7$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$1$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(-x + 1) + 3$. What is the value of $g(2)$?</p>","answer":["15"],"explanation":"$g(2) = f(-(2) + 1) + 3 = f(-1) + 3$. From the table, $f(-1) = 12$, so $g(2) = 12 + 3 = 15$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.c1952fd72c","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.transformed_table","prompt":"<table><tr><th>$x$</th><th>$f(x)$</th></tr><tr><td>$-2$</td><td>$2$</td></tr><tr><td>$-1$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$0$</td><td>$5$</td></tr><tr><td>$1$</td><td>$-7$</td></tr><tr><td>$2$</td><td>$-8$</td></tr><tr><td>$3$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr><tr><td>$4$</td><td>$-2$</td></tr></table><p>The table shows selected values of the function $f$. The function $g$ is defined by $g(x) = f(2x - 1) - 3$. What is the value of $g(0)$?</p>","answer":["-10"],"explanation":"$g(0) = f(2(0) - 1) - 3 = f(-1) - 3$. From the table, $f(-1) = -7$, so $g(0) = -7 - 3 = -10$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.6fa6dfe998","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(5, 3)$ and passes through the point $(3, 7)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 5$ and $q = 3$. Substitute the point: $7 = a(3 - (5))^2 + (3)$, so $4 = 4a$ and $a = 1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.72134701dc","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(4, -7)$ and passes through the point $(-4, 313)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["5"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 4$ and $q = -7$. Substitute the point: $313 = a(-4 - (4))^2 + (-7)$, so $320 = 64a$ and $a = 5$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.ac02284561","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(4, -9)$ and passes through the point $(-2, 63)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 4$ and $q = -9$. Substitute the point: $63 = a(-2 - (4))^2 + (-9)$, so $72 = 36a$ and $a = 2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.b656780905","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-2, -4)$ and passes through the point $(-4, -12)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -2$ and $q = -4$. Substitute the point: $-12 = a(-4 - (-2))^2 + (-4)$, so $-8 = 4a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.a6cbf3b50f","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-4, -2)$ and passes through the point $(-3, -4)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-2"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -4$ and $q = -2$. Substitute the point: $-4 = a(-3 - (-4))^2 + (-2)$, so $-2 = 1a$ and $a = -2$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.74cbe73928","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, -7)$ and passes through the point $(2, -154)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-3"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = -7$. Substitute the point: $-154 = a(2 - (-5))^2 + (-7)$, so $-147 = 49a$ and $a = -3$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.0b2eaaad56","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(5, -1)$ and passes through the point $(4, 0)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = 5$ and $q = -1$. Substitute the point: $0 = a(4 - (5))^2 + (-1)$, so $1 = 1a$ and $a = 1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.9887de351d","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-4, 3)$ and passes through the point $(-3, 2)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-1"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -4$ and $q = 3$. Substitute the point: $2 = a(-3 - (-4))^2 + (3)$, so $-1 = 1a$ and $a = -1$."},
{"id":"g.math.nonlinear-functions.3.4f81f7dd6c","section":"math","domain":"advanced","skill":"nonlinear-functions","difficulty":3,"tmpl":"math_hard.vertex_form_a","prompt":"The graph of the quadratic function $f$ in the $xy$-plane has its vertex at $(-5, -8)$ and passes through the point $(1, -62)$. If $f(x) = a(x - p)^2 + q$, where $a$, $p$, and $q$ are constants, what is the value of $a$?","answer":["-3/2","-1.5"],"explanation":"The vertex gives $p = -5$ and $q = -8$. Substitute the point: $-62 = a(1 - (-5))^2 + (-8)$, so $-54 = 36a$ and $a = -\\frac{3}{2}$."}
